# Question of Privilege: Explanation Concerning Omitted Information on the Transport Worker-Student Clash
Tamil title: (அவை உரிமை) - அரசு போக்குவரத்துத் துறைத் தொழிலாளர் - மாணவர் மோதல் பற்றி முதலமைச்சர் விளக்க உரையில் முழுத் தகவல் தரப்படாமை குறித்து விளக்கம்

Dated 28 March 1968

The Honourable C. N. Annadurai:

Mr Speaker, in the statement I made in the House under Rule 82, I provided the information that had reached me up to that time. Afterward, when the Leader of the Opposition in the Legislative Council asked a question as I went there, I mentioned in that House the information I had subsequently received. I referred to the firing.

I did not conceal or minimise it with the intention that it should be told only to that House and not to this one, or with the intention of withholding from this House information known to me. Everyone knows that I have the highest respect for both Houses. When I seek the cooperation of everyone in this House, it causes me distress even to think that our Leader of the Opposition might believe I would deliberately conceal certain matters.

I supplied the information available to me at that time. I sincerely regret that I was unable to provide the remaining information. The Leader of the Opposition said that students were in a position where they had to seek protection and that a succession of incidents had occurred. Through what I say in this House, I wish to assure the students that every kind of protection and reassurance will certainly be provided.

No one running this government would entertain the thought that students should be denied reassurance and protection. The country knows that no government would hold such a view. If the belief expressed by our Leader of the Opposition has spread among the student community, I wish to ask them with humility and affection to change it. I have deep concern for their welfare and will show no hesitation in seeking out and providing the protection due to them.

Our Leader of the Opposition also repeatedly said in this House, “There is no confidence.” Personally, and also in his capacity as a leading member of the Congress party, I have great confidence in the Leader of the Opposition, in every member of this House individually, and in the House collectively. I wish to say that the Leader of the Opposition should not even imagine that I acted because of any lack of confidence.

There was a tense atmosphere that day. He himself, while speaking on another matter, was unable to continue and stopped with his voice trembling. I was almost in the same condition. Because details and information did not arrive promptly, and because I had not been given complete information about the firing when I spoke, I could not mention it. It was not, as he suggested, because I intended to conceal it or because I wished to tell the Legislative Council but not this House.

Whenever information is received, it must be stated promptly in this House. I thank him for reminding me of the respect due to this House.

Mr K. Vinayagam:

Sir, the point made by our honourable Chief Minister, that he gave his statement on the basis of the information available to him, is correct. But when the statement was read at half past ten, the firing had already occurred. As far as we know, it occurred at three in the afternoon. There is no difference of opinion about the events of that day.

When we assembled yesterday and referred to this very matter, although all this had happened, not one detail was conveyed to us. We learnt it only by reading the newspapers. The Minister did not tell us that firing had occurred, how many people were wounded, what condition they were in, how many were affected, or how many students had died.

There are photographs of it. A rule says they may not be displayed, so I have not displayed them. Just as currency notes were once thrown into the House elsewhere, the rules say they cannot be thrown here. I am not interested in demonstrative politics. But I say that deliberately withholding all these details from us was not proper. It is “suppression of facts.” What do they call that?

The Honourable C. N. Annadurai:

That is precisely what I said. There was a tense atmosphere that day. It was solely because complete information was unavailable that day; there was not the slightest intention to conceal it or to say that it should not be told to this House. If the Leader of the Opposition or others hold such an impression, I respectfully ask them to change it.

I wholly agree with and support the principle that information should be conveyed as and when it becomes available. That is appropriate to parliamentary government, necessary, and just.

Mr P. G. Karuthiruman:

Mr Speaker, two matters have been left out of the present discussion. We are told that an order has been issued to search student hostels. We must also be told what arrangements the government is making to compensate poor students who were injured or beaten.

It is said that an order has been issued for searches, and the students have become agitated and afraid. Since bitterness arose between them and the transport workers, what action will be taken to ensure that nothing happens out of that bitterness when they go to college tomorrow, to restore goodwill, and to bring about reconciliation once again?

The Honourable C. N. Annadurai:

I shall take the last point first. In order to restore normal conditions, the government is considering appropriate means by which student representatives, transport-worker representatives, representatives of our government, leaders of various other parties, and all others who wish to participate may sit together and establish an atmosphere of sound reconciliation, preventing hostile feelings from spreading or arising. I wish to state that the government is considering the procedures required for this.

The other question concerned compensation for damaged property. If genuine damage or loss has occurred, this government accepts the principle that fair compensation should be provided. The details will be collected. When each damaged item is documented, there will be no hesitation in providing relief in a proper manner. I wish to make that clear.

---NOTES---
The source is a Legislative Assembly exchange, not a single uninterrupted speech. The English preserves the speaker changes among C. N. Annadurai, K. Vinayagam, and P. G. Karuthiruman.

The OCR subject line reads “அக்குலப்பு,” but the context and debate concern a clash or conflict between government transport workers and students. The English title uses “clash”; the Tamil source remains unchanged.

Large parts of Images 2 and 4 are fragmented in OCR. Direct scan inspection restores K. Vinayagam's speaker label, his English sentence “I am not interested in demonstrative politics,” Anna's reconciliation proposal, and the complete compensation ending.

The source juxtaposes a statement read at 10:30 with a firing said to have occurred at 3 p.m.; the precise sequence is ambiguous in the excerpt. The figures are translated as stated and not silently reconciled.

Claims about the clash, firing, casualties, searches, policing, and compensation are parliamentary statements translated in each speaker's voice and have not been independently verified.
